00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Phillip: In this video we are going to
look at the expectations of customers.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:10.360
So, what are customer expectations and why
is it important to meet or exceed them?

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:15.180
Maddy: Customer expectations are just
what they sound like, what the customer

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.520
expects from a service or a product.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.810
These will be formed by a culmination of
different factors, including experience with your

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:28.570
organisation, the opinions they’ve heard expressed
about the product or service you offer, and the

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.319
general reputation your organisation has overall.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:36.670
Phillip: Meeting and exceeding customer
expectations translates into loyal customers

00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:39.140
and an increase in word-of-mouth sales.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:44.469
Maddy: It’s important that you’re able to identify
exactly what the customer’s expectations are

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:50.110
and you can do this through formal channels like
surveys and feedback forms, or more informally

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.760
through natural conversations and observations.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.480
It’s not just about a flashy website or how
good an organisation looks on paper, it is

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:04.170
also about the face-to-face response from staff
that makes for a good customer experience as

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:07.019
well as meeting and exceeding expectations.

00:01:08.099 --> 00:01:11.380
Phillip: When you’ve established what
a customer expects, then you can then

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.120
start to plan how you’re going to
meet and exceed those expectations.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.229
Customers don’t just purchase products and
services they are often looking for a good

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:23.350
feeling, solutions to problems and good aftercare.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.809
Maddy: When a customer’s expectations
are met, it means that they have had

00:01:27.809 --> 00:01:32.170
the experience they wanted to have when
interacting with your organization and

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.250
purchasing the product or service you offer.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.600
This makes it more likely the customer
will probably become a return customer

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.119
themselves or may recommend you to others.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.160
Phillip: However, meeting expectations
of the customer is not as good

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.179
as exceeding their expectations.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:54.570
Although they may be happy that they got what they
wanted, by going that extra mile the experience

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:59.449
could be even better, which in turn would mean
that they are more likely to recommend you

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.060
and your services or products with confidence.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.770
Maddy: When a customer’s expectations
are exceeded, you might very well

00:02:06.779 --> 00:02:08.130
have made a customer for life!

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:13.299
When a customer makes the comment “my
expectations were exceeded” what they

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:15.940
are really saying is “I was impressed”.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:20.940
Exceeding their expectations will make
them feel like they can rely on you or your

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:26.019
organisation to always provide them with a
quality service and offering, boosting the

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:30.930
likelihood of them becoming a return customer and
recommending your organization to other people.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:35.690
Phillip: Unfortunately, though there may be
times when a customer may feel that their

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:41.349
expectations were simply not met, and this
may be for a lot of reasons, including the

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:46.800
possibility that the customer simply had
unrealistic expectations about what was possible.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:51.899
You should, however, always take these
situations where you a customer was left

00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:56.819
unsatisfied as learning opportunities, to
help improve your customer service, and the

00:02:56.819 --> 00:02:59.340
product and services your organisation offers.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.020
Maddy: Sometimes things may also go wrong
on your side, but it’s the way that you

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.990
handle things that makes all the difference.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.790
This often doesn’t involve a big monetary
investment instead good customer service and

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:16.679
personal interaction with a customer can help
with expectations and improve the provision.

00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:21.790
Phillip: What should always be remembered
is that without the customer, there would

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:26.260
not be an organisation as ultimately, they
are the ones that make it all possible.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:32.180
By consistently meeting and exceeding the
expectations of customers you can help guarantee

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:38.100
a stable, loyal, and growing customer base
which can in turn then help an organisation

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.820
expand and go from strength to strength.

